# Codex Adversarial Review

Target: working tree diff
Verdict: needs-attention

No-ship for Phase 2 as written: the plan over-promotes uncertain material to verified/objective layers, has contradictory scope accounting, and lacks a migration safety gate for moving or deleting staging content.

Findings:
- [high] Bulk verified promotion is under-specified and internally inconsistent (plan.md:561-622)
  P3 says there are 7 remaining source-needed files, lists 10 candidates, then says 8 files can be verified while naming only 7; Phase B then includes storm-surge/05 as B5 even though it was not in the P3 decision sentence. This makes the execution set ambiguous and creates a real risk that concept pages get marked verified by wrapper-level model citations rather than direct source support. In this repo, concepts/ is an objective layer requiring citations, so accidental verified status is a provenance defect, not just a planning typo.
  Recommendation: Replace P3/B with a single canonical table listing each exact file, required source_id/raw artifact, allowed claim scope, and target citation_status. Mark wrapper-only pages as source-needed or partial until the exact citations exist.
- [high] Manual catalog promotion treats provenance notes as verified model content without an artifact gate (plan.md:552-559)
  P2 promotes all adcirc/xbeach source catalog notes into models/<MODEL>/manual-notes/ and marks them verified because they are based on user + Codex analysis. That does not establish that each catalog entry has a stable raw/manual artifact, source_id, URL, checksum, or citation target. The likely failure is a set of objective-layer model notes that look canonical but cannot be audited back to the actual manual/repo/example they summarize.
  Recommendation: Split catalog entries into raw artifact indexes versus interpretive notes. Require per-note fields for source_id, raw path or upstream URL, captured date/checksum when applicable, and keep citation_status source-needed unless the referenced artifact is present and cited.
- [high] Phase A names copy-verify-archive but still allows lossy promote/delete semantics (plan.md:597-609)
  The section header says Phase A follows D2 copy-verify-archive, but the actual steps only say promote and allow README archive or delete. There is no manifest, checksum comparison, link rewrite check, rollback point, or validator gate before emptying _staging. Given the earlier 107-note promote history, repeating a simple mv/delete workflow can silently lose source context or break cross-links while making recovery expensive.
  Recommendation: Add an explicit migration manifest with source path, destination path, hash, classification decision, citation_status, and verification result. Copy first, run link/frontmatter validation, archive originals, and only delete staging in a separate final step after review.
- [medium] Failure-pattern and playbook classification defaults are too ambiguous for an objective-layer boundary (plan.md:539-550)
  The only rule for routing failure-patterns/playbooks is 'model mechanics vs user work method'. Mixed notes are likely: a model misuse pattern can cite source-code mechanics and also encode operational advice. With no default for mixed cases, a note can land in models/source-analysis and import user experience into the objective model layer, violating the repo rule that objective layers require cited sources and keeping personal experience in experience/.
  Recommendation: Define a triage checklist and default mixed notes to experience/ with source-needed, or split each note into a cited source-analysis fragment plus an experience/playbook fragment. Require the classification decision in the migration manifest.
- [medium] EFDC Chuksan quantitative experience has no verification gate in the executable phase (plan.md:624-630)
  The risk section correctly says the +-1.5 cm/yr and 15-year +-22 cm values should start source-needed, but Phase C lists the data source as 'user memory + EFDC source-analysis'. EFDC source-analysis can support model mechanics, not the site-specific quantitative result. Without an explicit gate, this note can be created with precise numbers that readers may treat as verified even though the raw run outputs are absent.
  Recommendation: In Phase C, require efdc-chuksan-sediment.md to be citation_status: source-needed, label the numeric values as user memory/anecdotal, and prohibit verified status until raw run outputs or reproducible derived tables are added.
- [medium] Phase 2 scope is too broad to complete without partial-state drift (plan.md:599-650)
  The plan combines ~46 staging promotes, 8 concept verifications, 3 new experience notes, source registry edits, tool docs, boundary changes, and INDEX final cleanup in one phase. That is a migration plus content verification plus policy cleanup. The failure mode is a half-finished working tree where staging is partly emptied while concept statuses and indexes claim completion, making later review unable to distinguish copied, verified, and merely moved content.
  Recommendation: Split into separate reviewable phases: A migration manifest/copy/archive only, B concept verification only, C experience notes only, and D index/policy cleanup after validators pass.

Next steps:
- Fix P3/B counts and verified criteria before touching concept files.
- Add a copy-verify-archive manifest and make deletion/archive a separate gated step.
- Keep user-memory quantitative notes source-needed until raw evidence exists.
